, "lean": -5.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 1.85}, "rating_margin": 1.5, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.22, "baseline_margin": 2.85, "analyst_adjustment": 1.15, "key_factors": ["Ager's roughly 15-to-1 fundraising advantage ($5.4M vs. $355K)", "Open seat after Edwards withdrew; markets price Ager near 80%", "Strong Democratic national environment offsets the R+5 district lean", "Thin, dated polling — one September matchup poll from a D-sponsored pollster"], "rationale": "Jamie Ager's enormous financial edge, the open seat created by Chuck Edwards' withdrawal, and prediction markets pricing him near 80 percent justify a clearer Democratic edge than the district's five-point Republican lean would suggest. The only public matchup poll shows a narrower three-point race, so the margin stays modest rather than runaway.", "watch": "Vice President Vance's Helene relief messaging in Hendersonville, late Republican outside spending, or turnout differences could close a gap built on thin polling.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "NC-11 is an open seat this year after Republican Chuck Edwards withdrew following his renomination, leaving Democrat Jamie Ager and Republican Jennifer Balkcom to compete in a district that leaned Republican by five points at the presidential level. Ager holds a large financial advantage, raising about $5.4 million to Balkcom's $355,000, and outside groups have spent against the Republican nominee while leaving the Democrat untouched. Betting markets on all three major platforms put Ager's chances near 80 percent.\n\nMost analysts call the race a tossup, though three outlets moved to a Democratic edge in late October. The only public matchup poll, from a Democratic-sponsored pollster in September, showed Ager up three. A strong national Democratic environment appears to be offsetting the district's Republican lean, and Vice President Vance's visit to Henders